**Clarify semantics.** Duplicate means the same UUID within the last 10 minutes. Decide: a dropped duplicate does *not* extend the window (the ID expires 10 minutes after its first accepted sighting). State this assumption.

**Size.** 10,000/s x 600 s = about 6 million live IDs. A UUID is 16 bytes of data, but boxed objects in a Java `HashMap` cost many times that (often 80-100 bytes per entry), so about 0.5 GB. Mention a compact alternative: store the two longs in a primitive open-addressing table.

**Design (hash map plus FIFO queue).**
- `Map<UUID, Long> seen` mapping id to first-seen time.
- `Deque<Entry(id, time)> order` ordered by time, because events arrive in time order.

```java
synchronized boolean accept(UUID id, long now) {
  evict(now);                         // amortised O(1)
  if (seen.containsKey(id)) return false;   // duplicate: drop
  seen.put(id, now);
  order.addLast(new Entry(id, now));
  return true;
}
void evict(long now) {
  while (!order.isEmpty() && order.peekFirst().time <= now - TTL) {
    seen.remove(order.pollFirst().id);
  }
}
```
Check-and-insert is O(1) expected; each ID is enqueued and removed once, so eviction is amortised O(1). Use a monotonic clock (`System.nanoTime`) so wall-clock jumps do not break expiry.

**Concurrency.** One global lock may be fine at 10k/s, but to scale shard by `hash(uuid) % N` with a lock, map and queue per shard (an ID always lands in the same shard). `ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent` gives an atomic check-and-insert; expiry can be done by a background thread or by time buckets (one set per minute, dropping whole buckets), which cuts per-entry bookkeeping.

**Limits.** State is lost on restart and not shared across instances. For multiple instances use Redis `SET key NX EX 600` or route by UUID hash to the same instance. A Bloom filter per bucket saves memory but allows false positives (wrongly dropped notifications), so state that trade-off.
